Title:
ttd-capa — CAPA-compatible capability extraction from Time Travel Debugging (TTD) traces

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- ttd-capa is a runtime capability extractor that converts Microsoft Time Travel Debugging (TTD) execution traces into a CAPA-compatible JSON feature report.  
- It addresses the gap where static CAPA analysis misses behavior hidden by packing/obfuscation by mining what the binary actually executed after unpacking.  
- The extractor walks module-load events, enumerates exported functions, then sweeps the trace to log calls into those exports including arguments and return values.  
- It decodes parameters using Microsoft Win32 API metadata (win32json), re-reading `[Out]` parameters at function return to capture filled buffers/handles, with a heuristic fallback when metadata is missing.  
- A Python wrapper (`ttd-capa.py`) runs extraction + CAPA rule matching (`capa -f ttd`) and a timeline tool (`ttd-timeline.py`) reconstructs capability/call order using TTD timestamps.  
- Useful primarily for malware analysts, reverse engineers, and threat intel teams triaging packed samples; also valuable for blue teams validating observed behaviors against capability rules.

Technical Focus:
- Microsoft Time Travel Debugging (TTD) trace parsing and replay APIs (TTDReplay.dll)
- CAPA feature extraction format and rule matching (capa -f ttd)
- Win32 API argument decoding via SDK metadata (win32json), including `[Out]` parameter re-reads
- Export-based call target identification across loaded modules
- Execution-order reconstruction using trace timestamps (timeline)

Use Cases:
- Triage packed/obfuscated malware by extracting post-unpack runtime capabilities
- Generate CAPA reports from dynamic execution without traditional API hooking
- Build behavior timelines (capabilities or raw API calls) for incident reports and reverse engineering
- Compare static vs runtime capability coverage to identify hidden functionality
- Enrich threat intel with concrete API-level evidence from recorded executions

Keywords:
Time Travel Debugging, TTD, WinDbg, TTDReplay.dll, TTDReplayCPU.dll, CAPA, capa rules, capability extraction, runtime analysis, packed malware, UPX, trace replay, Win32 API metadata, win32json, exported functions, argument decoding, out parameters, JSON report, timeline reconstruction, malware triage